          <p>
            <strong>T101 Connect.</strong> $200 less, five inches shorter, slower and rated 25 lb lower, but
            with JRNY adaptive workouts included. For walking only, and for users of average height, it does
            the job. Note the 2022 recall applied to T101-05 units, not the current Connect version.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>7.0 AT.</strong> $200 more at the prices we saw, with 15% incline, a heavier frame, watch
            pairing and three years of parts. Our <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon 7.0 AT review</a>
            covers it in full. For anyone who runs regularly, that is the better use of $200.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Outside Horizon.</strong> The <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6</a>,
            from the same parent company, and the machines in our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$1,000 guide</a>
            are worth pricing on the same day. The whole Horizon range is ranked in our
            <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best Horizon treadmills</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy the Horizon T202</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if you mainly walk and want a full-length deck.</strong> A 60-inch deck, 12 mph,
            a 325 lb rating and lifetime motor cover at $800 to $900 is the core case, and the 7.25-inch
            step-on height suits walkers who find taller decks awkward.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you jog occasionally.</strong> Both reviews treat light jogging as within its
            range.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step up to the 7.0 AT</strong> if you will run several times a week, are close to the
            weight limit, or want watch pairing; three years of parts is the reason.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Step down to the T101 Connect</strong> if you will only walk, are of average height, and
            want JRNY or the lower price.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pay the sale price, never the reference price,</strong> and check the delivered total and
            ship date before you order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Horizon T202 a good treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For walking and occasional jogging, yes, on its published figures and two independent reviews. It has a 20 by 60-inch deck, 0.5 to 12 mph, 12 incline levels, a 325 lb rating and lifetime frame and motor cover. Its weaker points are one year of parts cover and a 1.4 mm belt. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What motor does the Horizon T202 have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Horizon describes a Johnson Drive System motor but publishes no horsepower rating. Independent reviews give 2.75 HP or 2.75 CHP, which we cannot trace to Horizon. The motor is covered by a lifetime warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does the Horizon T202 cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 5 November 2026 Horizon listed it at $899 against a $1,299 reference price, expected to ship the week of 16 November. In mid-October it was $799 with a promotional code. Prices change with promotions, so compare against the best recent price rather than the reference price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the Horizon T101 and T202?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The T202 has a 60-inch deck instead of 55 inches, reaches 12 mph instead of 10, has 12 incline levels instead of 10, a 325 lb rating instead of 300 lb, and a 5-inch LCD. The T101 Connect is $200 cheaper and includes JRNY adaptive workouts. Both carry lifetime frame and motor and one-year parts and labour cover.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you run on a Horizon T202?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can jog and run on it; it reaches 12 mph on a 60-inch deck. Independent reviewers recommend it for walkers and occasional joggers and point regular runners to the Horizon 7.0 AT, which has three years of parts cover instead of one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Horizon T202 fold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. It folds with hydraulic assist from 76 inches long to 48 inches long and 62 inches tall, and it weighs 187 lb.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Horizon T202</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The T202 is a straightforward machine. These are the errors that cost buyers money or frustration.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring a deal against the $1,299 reference price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Horizon sold the T202 at $899 on 5 November 2026 and $799 with a code in October. A discount quoted from $1,299 is not a saving; compare with the best recent price and the delivered total.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it for regular running to save $200</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The T202 runs to 12 mph, but one year of parts cover and a thinner belt make the 7.0 AT the better buy for anyone running several times a week.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading a regional T202 listing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The T202-05, T202-26 and UK AtZone versions have different figures. Check that a listing or review describes the US T202, SKU HTM1386-01, before relying on it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging it into a GFCI outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Horizon says the T202 is not compatible with GFCI outlets, common in garages and basements. Check the outlet and use a dedicated circuit with no extension cord before delivery day.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

